## Notes for lesson2_slack Homework.
1. The script runs and sends whatever is inside the message field. In my case I edited my name. I could have edited the script to send any message I needed to relay.

2. The script essentially runs a loop with each severity level["INFO", "WARNING", "ERROR", "CRITICAL"]. For hw2_severity_tour.py, when the severity in the .env file is set to `SLACK_ALERT_SEVERITY=INFO`the script forwards all messages with severity INFO and above. Since INFO is the lowest severity, all messages are forwarded when this script is executed.
When the .env was edited with `SLACK_ALERT_SEVERITY=ERROR` only 2 messages were sent to slack. This happens because the .nv filters every message below  the "ERROR" setting in the .env file.

3. Servers speak UTC because it is universally accepted and unambiguous. There is no Daylight Savings time (spring forward or fall back) It is a standard and ALL important clocks speak it. In short, UTC is a fixed, atomic-clock-based reference (maintained by the BIPM from ~450 atomic clocks worldwide) that no local offset can perturb, making it the only sane common denominator for distributed systems.

My clock is currently 2:01PM PST and UTC is 22:01 UTC there is an 8-hour difference

4. The hw4_disk_watch, runs calculations based on the system's disk usage. Once it determines the usage level it sets a severity level based on that usage level and sends a message.
`if percent_used >= 90:
    severity = "ERROR"
elif percent_used >= 75:
    severity = "WARNING"
else:
    severity = "INFO"`
If the usage is >=90%, the severity level is set to ERROR
if the usage is >=75%, the severity level is set to WARNING
otherwise the severity level is set to INFO

A quiet INFO here is GOOD design because you don't want to be generating useless and annoying alerts. You don't want to be alerted when things are good; you want to be alerted when things are not working right.

5.The script checks the average load on the CPU(s) over the last 1, 5 and 15 minutes and sets the alert level to WARNING if the average cpu load over the last 5 minutes is greater than the cpu count. This means the server is busy.
The reason this would happen is if Balerica Bank gets bombarded by requests or even an attack.

6. The script generates a random incident with different severity levels. I believe a severity level of CRITICAL is enough to get someone out of bed at 3AM. All the other errors could just mean there are spikes in traffic and could self-resolve.


